## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP on grids with a minimax objective, reconstruction, and practical edge handling (blocked cells).

## Key Concepts
- DP on 2D grid with a minimax recurrence
- Path reconstruction via predecessor tracking
- Space optimization opportunities (1D row/column DP)
- INF handling for blockers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMaxRisk(r) {
  const n = r.length, m = r[0].length;
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(INF));
  const from = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = r[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) { dp[0][j] = Math.max(dp[0][j-1], r[0][j]); from[0][j] = 0; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) { dp[i][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][0], r[i][0]); from[i][0] = 1; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const up = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], r[i][j]);
      const left = Math.max(dp[i][j-1], r[i][j]);
      if (up <= left) { dp[i][j] = up; from[i][j] = 0; } else { dp[i][j] = left; from[i][j] = 1; }
    }
  }
  return {minMaxRisk: dp[n-1][m-1], parent: from};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow diagonal moves?
- How would you adapt for multiple starting points or 3D grids?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP state augmentation for a path with a constrained number of turns, combining path optimization with reconstruction. It demands careful handling of transitions when direction changes. 

## Key Concepts
- DP on grid paths with turn constraints
- 4D state dp[i][j][t][dir]
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time: O(n*m*k); Space: O(n*m*k)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () => Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG]))
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        const val = grid[i][j];
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], dp[i][j-1][t][0] + val);
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] + val);
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], dp[i-1][j][t][1] + val);
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] + val);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack using stored choices (omitted for brevity)
  return {dp, best: Math.max(...Array.from({length:K+1}, (_,t)=>Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][t][0], dp[n-1][m-1][t][1])))};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design for selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with reconstruction. Requires prefix sums, state definition, and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP over positions and count of subarrays
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Reconstructing intervals with predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs (O(nk) vs optimizations)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length; const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const from = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  for (let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best = -Infinity;
    let bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      if (dp[i-1][t-1] - P[i-1] > best){ best = dp[i-1][t-1] - P[i-1]; bestIdx = i-1; }
      const take = P[i] + best;
      if (take > dp[i-1][t]){ dp[i][t] = take; from[i][t] = [bestIdx+1, i]; }
      else { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction (omitted for brevity in this snippet)
  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], intervals: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle exactly k vs at most k subarrays?
- Can you reduce space to O(n) or O(k)?
- How would you reconstruct intervals efficiently without extra passes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design for special structure (palindromic substrings) and reconstruction under strict non-overlap.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic DP (isPal)
- 2D DP with exact k blocks
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalK(s, k, c){
  const n=s.length;
  const isPal=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(false));
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      if(s[i]===s[j] && (j-i<2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j]=true;
    }
  }
  // DP placeholders
  return null;
}
```

