## Why This Is Asked
The task tests a non-trivial DP where block value is a segment maximum, not sum. It requires precomputing segment maxima and careful reconstruction, exposing trade-offs between time and memory.

## Key Concepts
- DP on prefixes with exact block counts
- Transition uses max(A[j..i])
- Precompute M[j][i] in O(n^2)
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKBlocks(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const M=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ let m=-Infinity; for(let j=i;j>=0;j--){ m=Math.max(m,A[j]); M[j][i]=m; } }
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const split=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(k,i);t++){
      for(let j=1;j<=i;j++){
        const val=dp[j-1][t-1]+M[j-1][i-1];
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; split[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best:dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you optimize to reduce memory with rolling arrays?
- How does this extend when blocks must have minimum gap?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem evaluates proficiency in tree DP with disjoint-path packing and exact-path-count constraints, along with path reconstruction. It combines knapsack-on-tree techniques with path endpoint handling while guaranteeing vertex-disjointness.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with multiple states per node (unused vs. endpoint vs. interior)
- Knapsack-style merging across children via DP table convolution
- Path reconstruction through stored decision pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, parent) {
    const dp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
    dp0[u][0] = dp1[u][1] = 0;
    for (const v of children[u]) {
        dfs(v, u);
        const ndp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
        for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) for (let j = 0; j + i <= k; j++) {
            ndp0[i + j] = Math.max(ndp0[i + j], dp0[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp1[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp1[i + j] = Math.max(ndp1[i + j], dp1[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp2[i + j] = Math.max(ndp2[i + j], dp2[u][i] + dp0[v][j]);
        }
        dp0 = ndp0; dp1 = ndp1; dp2 = ndp2;
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic programming with a non-adjacency constraint and explicit reconstruction of the deletion indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index to enforce non-adjacency
- Exact deletions constraint (k) and reconstruction
- O(nk) time and O(nk) space (can optimize to O(k) with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumAfterDeletions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const keep = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const del = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  keep[0][0] = A[0];
  if (k >= 1) del[0][1] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      keep[i][t] = Math.max(keep[i-1][t], del[i-1][t]);
      if (keep[i][t] > NEG/2) keep[i][t] += A[i];
      if (t > 0) del[i][t] = keep[i-1][t-1];
    }
  }
  const end = Math.max(keep[n-1][k], del[n-1][k]);
  // Backtracking to collect deleted indices would be added here
  return {maxSum: end, deletedIndices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact deleted indices efficiently? 
- Can you reduce space to O(k) and still reconstruct? 
- How does the approach change if adjacent deletions are allowed at a cost?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over time with multiple constraints: multiple transactions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Two DP states: cash (not holding) and hold (holding).
- Transitions model buy/sell with transaction count t.
- Base cases initialize day 0; backtracking recovers trade days.
- Space optimization possible to O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfit(prices, k){
  const n = prices.length;
  if (n===0 || k===0) return 0;
  const cash = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const hold = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) cash[0][t] = (t===0)?0:-1e15;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++){
      cash[i][t] = cash[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) cash[i][t] = Math.max(cash[i][t], hold[i-1][t] + prices[i-1]);
      hold[i][t] = hold[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i][t], cash[i-1][t-1] - prices[i-1]);
    }
  }
  let best = 0;
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) best = Math.max(best, cash[n][t]);
  return best;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the exact trade days from the DP table?
- How does the solution change if k >= n/2 (unlimited transactions)?

## Why This Is Asked
Offers a new DP angle: path with exact turns, combining grid path DP with turn accounting. Tests deriving a tight state space, base cases, and reconstruction, mirroring routing-like constraints in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with an extra dimension for turns
- Two direction states to capture turns
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Careful base/edge-case handling (start cell, negative infinities)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  // 4D DP: [i][j][t][d]
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () =>
    Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])));
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        // last move right
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(
            (dp[i][j-1][t][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t][0] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
        // last move down
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(
            (dp[i-1][j][t][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t][1] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][K][0], dp[n-1][m-1][K][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k turns and also maximize path length if grid values included negative numbers?
- How would you optimize space to O(nmK) memory or better, and what would be the trade-offs for reconstruction?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of dynamic programming on prefixes with a non-crossing pairing constraint. The core challenge is encoding the no-crossing condition by considering the last pair ending at position i with its left partner p.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefixes**: Extra dimension tracks the number of pairs formed
- **Non-crossing constraint**: Enforced by pairing i with p < i and using dp[p-1][t-1], which ensures all pairs lie completely to the left of p
- **Reconstruction**: Requires storing backpointers to output the actual pairs
- **Complexity**: Time O(n²k), Space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPairs(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      prev[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let p = 1; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p - 1][t - 1] + A[p - 1] * A[i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skip: false, p };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t].skip) {
      i--;
    } else {
      const p = prev[i][t].p;
      pairs.push([p, i]);
      i = p - 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs: pairs.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with exact selection count and a non-adjacency constraint; includes reconstruction and edge handling for i-2. It also checks how to handle negative values and how to optimize space.

## Key Concepts

- 2D DP over i and t
- Non-adjacency via i-2 when taking i
- Reconstruct path from take flags
- Time/space complexity and rolling optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonAdjacentK(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      if(t>0){
        if(i>=2 && dp[i-2][t-1] !== NEG){
          const val = dp[i-2][t-1] + A[i-1];
          if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = true; }
        } else if(i===1 && dp[0][t-1] !== NEG){
          if(A[0] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = A[0]; take[i][t] = true; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
}
```

